text:
When sending a FormData body that includes a custom Node.js Readable stream (a stream that does not have a path property and is not an http.IncomingMessage), axios incorrectly sets a very small Content-Length header (only the multipart boundary overhead) instead of using Transfer-Encoding: chunked. This causes the request body to be truncated and the server to receive incomplete data.

To Reproduce
js
const axios = require('axios');
const FormData = require('form-data');
const { Readable } = require('stream');

// Create a custom readable stream (no path, no httpVersion)
const stream = Readable.from(Buffer.alloc(1024 * 1024)); // 1MB of data
const form = new FormData();
form.append('file', stream, { filename: 'test.bin' });

axios.post('http://httpbin.org/post', form, {
  headers: form.getHeaders(),
}).then(res => console.log(res.data)).catch(console.error);
Observed behavior:
The request is sent with Content-Length: ~271 (the multipart overhead) instead of Transfer-Encoding: chunked. The server only receives the first 271 bytes; the rest of the stream data is lost or the connection hangs.

Expected behavior:
Since the stream length cannot be determined, axios should not set a Content-Length header and should fall back to chunked transfer encoding, as it did in older versions (e.g., 1.1.3).

Axios Version
1.7.x (tested up to 1.19) – regression introduced after 1.1.3.

Additional context
The root cause is in the getLength / _trackLength logic. When a value is a Readable stream, it is considered measurable only if it has a path property (file stream) or is an HTTP response (value.readable && value.hasOwnProperty('httpVersion')). For any other stream, _trackLength sets valueLength to 0 and does not add it to _valuesToMeasure. Later, if _valuesToMeasure is empty, getLength immediately returns _overheadLength + _valueLength, which is just the boundary overhead. Axios then treats this as a valid, known length and sets Content-Length.

This is a regression compared to axios 1.1.3, where no Content-Length was proactively calculated for FormData with streams, so the underlying HTTP layer correctly used chunked encoding.

A possible fix would be: if any stream is encountered that cannot be measured, getLength should return null (or an error) to signal that the total length is unknown, forcing axios to send the request without Content-Length.

## Describe the issue

axios contradicts itself about what `status === 0` means, and Firefox 152 made the disagreement reachable.

The XHR adapter has the rule right, and scopes the `file:` exception properly:

```js
// lib/adapters/xhr.js, the onreadystatechange fallback
// The request errored out and we didn't get a response, this will be
// handled by onerror instead
// With one exception: request that using file: protocol, most browsers
// will return status as 0 even though it's a successful request
if (request.status === 0 && !(request.responseURL && request.responseURL.startsWith('file:'))) {
  return;
}
```

`settle()` does not scope it at all. It resolves any status-0 response as a success, on any scheme:

```js
// lib/core/settle.js
if (!response.status || !validateStatus || validateStatus(response.status)) {
  resolve(response);
}
```

The catch is where that adapter guard lives. It sits in the `onreadystatechange` fallback, which only runs when `'onloadend' in request` is false, and that is never the case in a current browser. Every evergreen browser takes the `onloadend` path instead, which has no equivalent check, so the unscoped version in `settle()` is the one that actually runs.

For about six years this did not matter, because browsers always fired `error` or `abort` before `loadend` on a failed or canceled XHR. `onabort`/`onerror` would reject and set `request = null`, and `onloadend` would bail on `if (!request) return` before `settle()` ever ran.

Firefox 152 changed that. [Bugzilla 1505389](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1505389) made navigation-canceled XHRs fire only `readystatechange` and `loadend`, with no `abort` and no `error`. The patch says it outright:

> Don't make navigation block XHR and call onerror/abort
>
> Fires readystatechange and loadend intead of abort

It is intentional and it has been upstreamed. Mozilla landed a conformance test in web-platform-tests ([wpt#59603](https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/pull/59603), `xhr/abort-during-navigation.window.js`) asserting that `error`, `abort` and `load` must not fire. Chrome already fires nothing and passes it. Safari still fires `abort` and would fail it. So this looks like where the platform is heading rather than a Firefox quirk that will get reverted.

Note also that `!response.status` is evaluated before `validateStatus`, so this cannot be worked around with config.

## Example code

Any request that is in flight when the user navigates away, on Firefox 152 or later:

```js
// Page navigates (link click, location assignment, reload) while this is pending.
const { data } = await axios.get('/api/things');
// Firefox <= 151: rejects with ECONNABORTED, because the adapter saw an abort event.
// Firefox >= 152: RESOLVES, with status 0 and data === ''.
// Anything parsing or validating `data` then fails on an empty string.
```

Reproducible without a browser, by driving the real xhr adapter with a stub `XMLHttpRequest`. The only difference between the two runs is whether `error` fires:

```js
let SEQUENCE = [];
globalThis.XMLHttpRequest = class {
  constructor() {
    this.readyState = 0; this.status = 0; this.statusText = '';
    this.responseText = ''; this.response = ''; this.timeout = 0;
    this.onloadend = this.onabort = this.onerror = this.ontimeout = this.onreadystatechange = null;
  }
  open() {} setRequestHeader() {} abort() {} getAllResponseHeaders() { return ''; }
  send() {
    setImmediate(() => {
      this.readyState = 4;
      for (const ev of SEQUENCE) this[`on${ev}`]?.call(this, { type: ev });
    });
  }
};
const { default: axios } = await import('axios');

for (const [label, seq] of [
  ['error precedes loadend (every browser before FF152)', ['readystatechange', 'error', 'loadend']],
  ['loadend only        (Firefox >= 152 nav-cancel)     ', ['readystatechange', 'loadend']],
]) {
  SEQUENCE = seq;
  const out = await axios.get('https://example.com/x', { adapter: 'xhr' })
    .then((r) => `RESOLVED status=${r.status} data=${JSON.stringify(r.data)}`)
    .catch((e) => `rejected  ${e.code}`);
  console.log(label, '->', out);
}
```

Output on axios 1.18.1:

```
error precedes loadend (every browser before FF152) -> rejected  ERR_NETWORK
loadend only        (Firefox >= 152 nav-cancel)     -> RESOLVED status=0 data=""
```

Confirmed in a real Firefox 153.0 as well, using the same iframe technique as Mozilla's WPT test. An in-flight XHR is canceled by a document navigation and the surviving parent records the events plus axios's outcome:

```json
{"ua":"Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10.15; rv:153.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/153.0",
 "events":["loadend(st=0)"],
 "firedAbort":false, "firedError":false, "firedLoad":false,
 "axios":{"outcome":"RESOLVED","status":0,"dataType":"string","data":"\"\""}}
```

## Expected behavior

A request that never received a response should reject, as it did on Firefox 151 and as it still does in every other engine. Reads over `file:`, which some environments report as status 0 on success, should keep resolving.

## Actual behavior

It resolves as a success carrying `status: 0` and `data: ''`. No error is thrown, so the failure surfaces later as a parsing or validation error on an empty string, far from its cause.

## Environment

- Axios version: 1.18.1 (behavior present since 0.19.1, when #2470 landed)
- Adapter: xhr
- Runtime: Firefox 152 and later, verified on Firefox Developer Edition 153.0. Chrome and Safari are unaffected today, though Safari changes if it adopts wpt#59603
- OS: macOS 15
- Additional context: browser build, default adapter, no custom `validateStatus`

:surfer: Investigated and written with AI assistance, reviewed before posting.

### Describe the bug

`InterceptorManager` stores interceptors in a plain array (`this.handlers`). When `eject(id)` is called, it sets the slot to `null` rather than removing it:

In applications that frequently add and remove interceptors (e.g., per-request auth interceptors, retry logic), this causes unbounded growth of the `handlers` array with `null` entries. Every request dispatch must filter these out.

### Steps to reproduce

const instance = axios.create();

for (let i = 0; i < 10_000; i++) {
  const id = instance.interceptors.request.use((cfg) => cfg);
  instance.interceptors.request.eject(id);
}

// handlers array now has 10,000 null slots
console.log(instance.interceptors.request.handlers.length); // 10000

### Expected behavior

The `handlers` array should not grow unboundedly. Ejected interceptors should not leave behind `null` tombstones that accumulate over the lifetime of an axios instance.

### Suggested fix

Replace the Array with a Map keyed by an incrementing integer ID:

constructor() {
  this.handlers = new Map();
  this._idCounter = 0;
}

use(fulfilled, rejected, options) {
  const id = this._idCounter++;
  this.handlers.set(id, {
    fulfilled,
    rejected,
    synchronous: options?.synchronous ?? false,
    runWhen: options?.runWhen ?? null,
  });
  return id;
}

eject(id) {
  this.handlers.delete(id);
}

This is a non-breaking change — the returned ID is still a number and eject(id) still works the same way from the caller's perspective.

### Environment

- axios version: v1.x (latest)
- Affected file: lib/core/InterceptorManager.js

## Bug Description

`AxiosHeaders.get(name, true)` internally calls `parseTokens` to split a structured header value into its token/parameter pairs. When a parameter value is a **quoted-string** (wrapped in `""`) as permitted by RFC 9110 §5.6.4, the double-quote delimiters are incorrectly included in the returned value.

## Reproduction

```js
import axios from 'axios';

// Simulate a response with a quoted boundary parameter (common in multipart responses)
const headers = new axios.AxiosHeaders();
headers.set('content-type', 'multipart/form-data; boundary="----=_Part_123"');

const tokens = headers.get('content-type', true);
console.log(tokens.boundary);
// Actual:   '----=_Part_123'   ← includes the surrounding DQUOTE characters
// Expected: '----=_Part_123'     ← DQUOTE delimiters must be stripped per RFC 9110
```

## Related second issue

The regex `[^,;]+` used in `parseTokens` also captures trailing whitespace before a delimiter. So `charset=utf-8   ;` yields `'utf-8   '` instead of `'utf-8'`.

## Root Cause

In `lib/core/AxiosHeaders.js`, the `parseTokens` function assigns `match[2]` (the raw captured token value) directly to `tokens[match[1]]` without:
1. Stripping surrounding double-quotes (required by RFC 9110 §5.6.4)
2. Trimming trailing whitespace

## Impact

Any code that uses `headers.get('content-type', true)` and checks `tokens.boundary` will receive the value with quotes, causing string comparison failures. This affects multipart form data processing where the `Content-Type` boundary is frequently quoted by browsers and servers.

## Expected Behavior

Per [RFC 9110 §5.6.4](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110#section-5.6.4):
> A quoted-string is a sequence of zero or more characters enclosed within double-quotes. The initial and final DQUOTE characters are not part of the value.

use case: custom `AxiosRequestConfig` field

```ts
import axios from 'axios';

export const someFlag = Symbol('some flag used in request interceptor')

declare module 'axios' {
  export interface AxiosRequestConfig {
    [someFlag]?: boolean
  }
}

await axios.get('/api/dashboard', { [someFlag]: true });
```

but current `mergeConfig` is using [`Object.keys`](https://github.com/axios/axios/blob/v1.17.0/lib/core/mergeConfig.js#L114) which is `owned enumerable string keys`, symbol keys are not included in `Object.keys`, cause the configured value always `undefined` in interceptors

When using this package in a Next.js application, it seems like the bundler is adding the `buffer` node module so it is available in the browser.

This package only uses the Buffer when [converting to form data](https://github.com/axios/axios/blob/a8e4f13aeecc45a3b8fab3ecfd9ddb5d70fb772b/lib/helpers/toFormData.js#L141). We can look into alternatives that don't include the buffer module, like Uint8Array or similar browser-native APIs.

---

To reproduce the issue:

Create a Next.js application from a template

```bash
# Create app
yarn create next-app analyze-bundles-app

# Navigate to the directory
cd analyze-bundles-app

# Install the module
yarn axios
```


Update `app/page.tsx` to use the module

```tsx
"use client";
import axios from "axios";
import { useEffect } from "react";

export default function Home() {
  useEffect(() => {
    axios
      .get("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1")
      .then((response) => {
        console.log(response.data);
      });
  }, []);
  return <div>hello world</div>;
}

```

Make a production build with the analyzer plugin

```bash
yarn next experimental-analyze
```

When the reports are created, open http://localhost:4000 and see that the buffer module is included in the bundle.

<img width="1764" height="1132" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/580b6589-d19d-4eb5-a4ee-cd06383c69fc" />

In 1.16.0 and prior a call with proxy would succeed if the certificate were self signed as long as either rejectUnauthorized was overridden to false or the certificate itself was set.

```
const options = {
    method: 'GET',
    url: `https://mydomain.com`,
    proxy: {
      protocol: 'https',
      host: '127.0.0.1',
      port: '8080'
    },
    httpsAgent: new https.Agent({
      rejectUnauthorized: false,
      ca: certificateData
    })
  };

  const response = await axios.request(options);
```

In 1.16.1, regardless of these values, you are met with a "self-signed certificate in certificate chain" error.

With the https-proxy-agent changes in 1.16.1, is there a different way to either provide the cert or have it ignored for a specific request?

### Describe the bug

With the http adapter and `maxRedirects: 0`, configured `timeout` values are ignored while the TCP connect phase is still pending. If the host's SYN is blackholed or the address is unroutable, the request only settles when the kernel's TCP retry gives up — roughly 5s on macOS, longer on Linux — regardless of how short `timeout` is.

`maxRedirects > 0` is unaffected, because the follow-redirects transport starts its own timer at request construction.

### Reproduction

Tested against current v1.x HEAD:

```js
import axios from 'axios';

// 192.0.2.1 is TEST-NET-1 (RFC 5737); kernel drops the SYN.
const url = 'http://192.0.2.1/';

async function attempt(label, config) {
  const t0 = Date.now();
  try { await axios.get(url, config); }
  catch (err) {
    console.log(`[${label}] ${Date.now() - t0}ms code=${err.code}`);
  }
}

await attempt('maxRedirects=0, timeout=500',  { maxRedirects: 0, timeout: 500 });
await attempt('maxRedirects=0, timeout=2000', { maxRedirects: 0, timeout: 2000 });
await attempt('maxRedirects=5, timeout=500',  { maxRedirects: 5, timeout: 500 });
```

Output (Node 24, macOS):

```
[maxRedirects=0, timeout=500]  5006ms code=ECONNABORTED
[maxRedirects=0, timeout=2000] 5002ms code=ECONNABORTED
[maxRedirects=5, timeout=500]   506ms code=ECONNABORTED
```

### Expected

The configured timeout should fire during the connect phase too, not only after the socket is established.

### Root cause

`ClientRequest.setTimeout(ms, cb)` from Node's native http module arms a socket-inactivity timer. That timer doesn't tick until the socket is bound and connected. If connect never completes, the timer never starts and the reject is driven by whatever connect error the kernel surfaces.

follow-redirects runs its own wall-clock timer that starts at request construction, which is why `maxRedirects > 0` honors the configured value immediately.

### Suggested fix

For the `maxRedirects === 0` path in `lib/adapters/http.js`, attach a wall-clock `setTimeout(config.timeout)` from the moment the request is created, fire the existing timeout error when it elapses, and clear it on `close` / `response` / successful settle. The existing message and code resolution can be reused so `timeoutErrorMessage` and `transitional.clarifyTimeoutError` keep working.

### Related

- Clarified scope of #6801
- PR #10657 attempted a broader fix that didn't address the connect-phase root cause
- Relevant locations: `lib/adapters/http.js:740` (transport selection), `lib/adapters/http.js:1023` (current `req.setTimeout` handler)

### Axios version

v1.x HEAD (verified at 25387ae)

### Adapter

http

## Background

`lib/helpers/composeSignals.js` currently composes multiple `AbortSignal`s and an optional timeout using manually-attached `addEventListener('abort', ...)` listeners. Modern runtimes (Node 19+, current browsers) provide `AbortSignal.any(...)` natively, which would let us drop the manual aggregation.

A previous attempt to do this (#7380) regressed behavior, so it was closed in favor of this issue.

## The constraint to preserve

Today, every reason that lands on the composed signal is normalized:

```js
const err = reason instanceof Error ? reason : this.reason;
controller.abort(
  err instanceof AxiosError
    ? err
    : new CanceledError(err instanceof Error ? err.message : err)
);
```

So `composedSignal.reason` is always an `AxiosError` (since `CanceledError` extends `AxiosError`).

The fetch adapter relies on this invariant at `lib/adapters/fetch.js:415` to recover from Safari's broken DOMException-like aborts:

```js
if (composedSignal && composedSignal.aborted && composedSignal.reason instanceof AxiosError) {
  const canceledError = composedSignal.reason;
  // ...
  throw canceledError;
}
```

A naive switch to `AbortSignal.any` propagates the source signal's reason as-is, breaking this for any user that calls `controller.abort()` with no reason or with a plain `Error`.

## What needs to happen

Use `AbortSignal.any` internally as the aggregator, but expose an outer signal whose reason is always an `AxiosError`/`CanceledError`. Approximate shape:

```js
if (typeof AbortSignal !== 'undefined' && typeof AbortSignal.any === 'function') {
  const outer = new AbortController();
  let timer;
  const clearTimer = () => { timer && clearTimeout(timer); timer = null; };

  const sources = [...signals];
  if (timeout) {
    const timeoutCtrl = new AbortController();
    timer = setTimeout(() => {
      timeoutCtrl.abort(new AxiosError(`timeout of ${timeout}ms exceeded`, AxiosError.ETIMEDOUT));
    }, timeout);
    sources.push(timeoutCtrl.signal);
  }

  const inner = AbortSignal.any(sources);
  inner.addEventListener('abort', () => {
    if (outer.signal.aborted) return;
    const r = inner.reason;
    outer.abort(
      r instanceof AxiosError
        ? r
        : new CanceledError(r instanceof Error ? r.message : r)
    );
  });

  outer.signal.unsubscribe = () => utils.asap(clearTimer);
  return outer.signal;
}

// existing fallback below
```

Keep the existing implementation as the fallback for runtimes without `AbortSignal.any`.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Fast path uses `AbortSignal.any` when available
- [ ] Fallback remains for runtimes without it
- [ ] `composedSignal.reason instanceof AxiosError` holds in every abort scenario, including:
  - timeout fires
  - `controller.abort()` with no reason
  - `controller.abort(new Error('x'))`
  - `controller.abort('string reason')`
  - `controller.abort(new AxiosError(...))`
  - cancelToken-derived signal
- [ ] `composedSignal.unsubscribe` is exposed and clears the timeout timer
- [ ] Regression test in `tests/unit/composeSignals.test.js` covering the four `controller.abort(...)` shapes above, asserting `signal.reason instanceof AxiosError`
- [ ] Fetch-adapter integration test confirming the Safari catch-block path (`fetch.js:415`) still triggers under user-initiated cancellation

## Context

- Original PR: #7380
- Safari catch-block origin: #10806
- Affected file: `lib/helpers/composeSignals.js`
- Caller relying on the invariant: `lib/adapters/fetch.js`

### Summary

`encode()` in `lib/helpers/buildURL.js` deliberately decodes `%3A`, `%24`, `%2C` and `%20` back to `:`, `$`, `,` and `+` for readability. Those characters are valid in a query component under RFC 3986, so the output is correct, but some backends require strict percent-encoding and reject the readable form.

axios already supports this case via `paramsSerializer.encode`, but it isn't documented with a strict-encoding example. Users hitting this run into it as a surprise (see the recurring stream of issues and PRs around `%3A`, e.g. #7290), and the workaround they often reach for is a one-off patch to `encode()` rather than the supported escape hatch.

### What to add to the docs

A short subsection under `paramsSerializer` showing the strict-encoding case:

```js
// Per-request: emit RFC 3986 strict percent-encoding for query values
axios.get('/foo', {
  params: { filter: JSON.stringify({ startedAt: '2025-01-23' }) },
  paramsSerializer: { encode: encodeURIComponent }
});

// Or set it on the instance defaults
const client = axios.create({
  paramsSerializer: { encode: encodeURIComponent }
});
```

Plus a one-line note explaining *why* the default decodes `:`, `$`, `,`, `+`: those characters are valid in the query component per RFC 3986, axios prefers the readable form, but stricter servers may require full encoding via the option above.

### Where

`README.md` `paramsSerializer` section, and the same section in `docs/` if it lives there separately.

### Related

- #7290 (closed) — proposed an in-encoder JSON-shape heuristic; the right answer is the existing `paramsSerializer.encode` hook.

### Summary

`utils.toJSONObject` currently uses a path-based stack for cycle detection (`lib/utils.js:753-786`). That works, but it's `O(n)` per check via `Array.indexOf` and the cleanup-on-ascent logic is easy to get wrong (see #7230, which dropped the cleanup and silently broke DAG handling).

We should rewrite this to use a `WeakSet` while preserving path semantics — `add` on descent, `delete` on ascent — and add coverage for the shared-sibling case so future refactors can't regress it.

### Why path semantics matter

The current implementation only treats an object as "seen" if it's an ancestor on the current recursion path. This is deliberate: shared sibling references (a non-cyclic DAG) need to be serialized at every occurrence, not deduplicated.

```js
const shared = { a: 1 };
toJSONObject({ x: shared, y: shared });
// expected: { x: { a: 1 }, y: { a: 1 } }
```

A naive `WeakSet` swap that omits `delete` on ascent turns the second occurrence into `undefined`, which the caller then drops entirely. That's the regression #7230 introduced.

### Proposed change

In `lib/utils.js`:

```js
const toJSONObject = (obj) => {
  const visited = new WeakSet();

  const visit = (source) => {
    if (isObject(source)) {
      if (visited.has(source)) return;
      if (isBuffer(source)) return source;

      if (!('toJSON' in source)) {
        visited.add(source);
        const target = isArray(source) ? [] : {};
        forEach(source, (value, key) => {
          const reducedValue = visit(value);
          !isUndefined(reducedValue) && (target[key] = reducedValue);
        });
        visited.delete(source);
        return target;
      }
    }
    return source;
  };

  return visit(obj);
};
```

### Test coverage to add

`tests/unit/utils.test.js` `describe('toJSON', ...)` currently only exercises self-cycles. Add at minimum:

- Shared sibling object referenced twice — both occurrences should serialize.
- Shared sibling array referenced twice — same.
- Mixed case: shared sibling that itself contains a self-cycle — outer occurrences serialize, inner cycle is broken.

### Related

- #7230 — closed/will-be-closed; this issue tracks the maintainer-led version.
